@title How a 911 Call Is Received and Dispatched
@subtitle Marrow County Emergency Communications District
@author Public Safety Systems team
@date 2026-09-30

## Overview

911 calls arrive from the telephone carriers into the primary dispatch center. A backup dispatch center across the county can take over if the primary is lost.

!! The backup center is activated only when the shift supervisor declares the primary unavailable. It never activates automatically.

## Call path

diagram callpath dir=LR title="911 call path and failover"
callpath: callers "Callers" user
callpath: carriers "Telecom Carriers" ext
callpath: gis "State GIS Service" ext
callpath: field "Field Units" team
callpath: router "Call Router" svc
callpath: cada "CAD Server A" svc "Computer-aided dispatch, active" focus
callpath: cadb "CAD Server B" svc "Hot standby"
callpath: radio "Radio Gateway" svc
callpath: srouter "Standby Call Router" svc
callpath: replica "CAD Replica" db
callpath: callers -> carriers : voice and caller location
callpath: carriers -> router : SIP trunks
callpath: router -> cada : call record
callpath: cada -> cadb : state sync
callpath: cada --> replica : asynchronous replication
callpath: router --> srouter : heartbeat
callpath: cada -> gis : address lookup
callpath: cada -> radio : dispatch message
callpath: radio -> field : P25 radio
callpath: group primary "Primary Dispatch Center" router, cada, cadb, radio
callpath: group backup "Backup Dispatch Center" srouter, replica
callpath: legend

## Failover targets

table = Component,Target; Call routing,Switches to the Standby Call Router within **30 seconds**; CAD Server B,Takes over within **2 minutes**; CAD Replica,Lags the primary by under **5 seconds**; Backup center,Fully staffed and taking calls within **10 minutes**

! Failover to the backup center is a human decision: the shift supervisor must declare the primary unavailable first.
